    content: "# Origins of the Cold War\n\n*Grade 10 U.S. & World History · Unit 5 of 10 · Cold War & Decolonization · Lesson 1*\n\n## Objective\n\n**I can** explain ideological and geopolitical rivalry after 1945.\n\n**Teacher focus:** Students source documents and argue with evidence. Prefer multi-causal explanations.\n\n## Warm-up (3–5 minutes)\n\nExamine a short primary-source snippet (teacher-provided). Who produced it, when, and for what purpose? Write one sentence.\n\nShare: Which historical thinking habit from Unit 5 helps with “Origins of the Cold War”?\n\n## Teach\n\n### Big idea\n\nCold War rivalry and decolonization remade sovereignty after 1945. Today's focus — **Origins of the Cold War** — develops that habit inside **Cold War & Decolonization**.\n\n### Why it matters\n\nMany current borders and alliances come from this era. At Prosper Prep we train civic-ready historical reasoning: source first, argue carefully, revise when evidence conflicts.\n\n### Language bank\n\n- **containment** — define it; use it in a sourced sentence.\n- **proxy** — define it; use it in a sourced sentence.\n- **decolonization** — define it; use it in a sourced sentence.\n- **deterrence** — define it; use it in a sourced sentence.\n\n### Worked example A\n\n**Step 1 — Source** a document related to “Origins of the Cold War.”\n**Step 2 — Claim** one historical argument (not a slogan).\n**Step 3 — Evidence** with a short quotation or concrete paraphrase.\n**Step 4 — Limitation** — what can't this source prove?\n**Step 5 — Check** against: Bipolar cartoons that erase local actors, or inevitability narratives.\n\n**Sample narrative:** “I sourced the author and audience, claimed a multi-causal explanation, corroborated with a second document, and noted what remains uncertain.”\n\n### Worked example B\n\nSwitch genres (map, letter, treaty). Keep sourcing + claim + limitation.\n\n### Common mistakes\n\n- **Watch for:** Bipolar cartoons that erase local actors, or inevitability narratives.\n- **Also watch for:** presentism; single-cause myths; quoting without context.\n- **Repair move:** Re-source, then rewrite the claim to match what the documents can actually support.\n\n### Connect to prior learning\n\nWorld wars, Cold War, and U.S. turning points require corroboration across global and domestic scales.\n\n## Guided practice (we do)\n\n1. Source a shared document as a class; write one claim + one limitation.\n2. Corroborate with a second source; revise the claim.\n3. Error hunt: repair a single-cause paragraph.\n\n## Independent practice\n\nComplete each item with sourcing and evidence.\n\n1. Practice 1: Using a newspaper editorial related to “Origins of the Cold War,” (1) source it (who/when/purpose), (2) state one claim the document supports, (3) name one limitation or bias, (4) connect to a larger Unit 5 development in 3–5 sentences.\n2. Practice 2: Using a map of alliances or trade routes related to “Origins of the Cold War,” (1) source it (who/when/purpose), (2) state one claim the document supports, (3) name one limitation or bias, (4) connect to a larger Unit 5 development in 3–5 sentences.\n3. Practice 3: Using a political cartoon from the era related to “Origins of the Cold War,” (1) source it (who/when/purpose), (2) state one claim the document supports, (3) name one limitation or bias, (4) connect to a larger Unit 5 development in 3–5 sentences.\n4. Practice 4: Using a treaty excerpt related to “Origins of the Cold War,” (1) source it (who/when/purpose), (2) state one claim the document supports, (3) name one limitation or bias, (4) connect to a larger Unit 5 development in 3–5 sentences.\n5. Practice 5: Using a diary letter from a civilian related to “Origins of the Cold War,” (1) source it (who/when/purpose), (2) state one claim the document supports, (3) name one limitation or bias, (4) connect to a larger Unit 5 development in 3–5 sentences.\n6. Practice 6: Using a government proclamation related to “Origins of the Cold War,” (1) source it (who/when/purpose), (2) state one claim the document supports, (3) name one limitation or bias, (4) connect to a larger Unit 5 development in 3–5 sentences.\n\n### Answer key (quality bar)\n\n1. Sourcing is specific; claim is evidenced; limitation is honest; connection names causes/effects or continuity/change — not a moral slogan alone.\n2. Sourcing is specific; claim is evidenced; limitation is honest; connection names causes/effects or continuity/change — not a moral slogan alone.\n3. Sourcing is specific; claim is evidenced; limitation is honest; connection names causes/effects or continuity/change — not a moral slogan alone.\n4. Sourcing is specific; claim is evidenced; limitation is honest; connection names causes/effects or continuity/change — not a moral slogan alone.\n5. Sourcing is specific; claim is evidenced; limitation is honest; connection names causes/effects or continuity/change — not a moral slogan alone.\n6. Sourcing is specific; claim is evidenced; limitation is honest; connection names causes/effects or continuity/change — not a moral slogan alone.\n\n## Exit ticket\n\n1. One-sentence claim from today's lesson with a source type named.\n2. One limitation of your best piece of evidence.\n3. One connection to an earlier unit.\n\n## Wrap-up\n\nHistory done well is **sourced argument**. Next lesson continues Unit 5: Cold War & Decolonization.\n",
